      "strings": {
        "brief": "You're on an authorised exercise at Carrholme Services, a fictional firm. You are covering the Care Laundry desk this morning. Items are waiting in the inbox: some are routine, some are requests or complaints that belong with someone else, and one or two were made to trick you. Sort them, and check or report anything that doesn't sit right. Reporting or checking when you are unsure is always fine and never marked down, though a real one does take a colleague's time to check.",
        "defendIntro": "Take the Care Laundry desk inbox one item at a time and decide what to do with each. Anything about someone's personal data, a request to see, correct or delete it, or a breach, goes to the DPO the same day; report or check anything that doesn't sit right. There's no clock on you here.",
        "mondayCard": "If personal data goes somewhere it should not, or someone asks to see, correct or delete their data, or complains about how we used it, tell your DPO the same day. Do not wait to be sure, and do not try to fix it alone.",
        "noBlameLine": "Mistakes with personal data happen to careful people, usually when they are busy. What turns a mistake into harm is silence. Nobody at Carrholme is in trouble for reporting, including their own slip.",
        "defendOnly": "Any of these can catch a careful person on a busy morning. The ones you were caught out by are shown below, with what good looks like. Reporting fast is what limits the harm.",
        "conceptPanel": {
          "heading": "Behind every row is a person",
          "text": "Behind every row in a spreadsheet is a person. If it goes wrong, tell the DPO straight away."
        }
      },
      "debrief": {
        "mondayCard": "slot:customer_report_route",
        "noBlameLine": true
      }
    },
    "awareness": {
      "ruleOfThumb": {
        "heading": "The habit to keep",
        "text": "When a message pressures you to move money, grant access, sign in or read back a code, stop and check it yourself through a route you already trust, not the link, number or page the message gives you. Taking a minute to confirm is always a fair thing to do."
      }
    }
